Z||000000||BlackARROW||04-30-2001||08:29 PM||||I don't know, but I think my topic is related in some part with the "Cold Intake" by Formula88.<P>My Thermac (thermostatic air cleaner) isn't working very well I guess.  I think it's the problem I was looking for some time ago.  When I'm reving it up on 3 to 4k rpm, it's whisling.  My question is, could it damage anything if I'd stuck that damper door to get only cold air? and not try to get something out from around the manifold?  Or what's the purpose of that thing?   I kindda like that "turbo"-like sound when I'm reving high, but I just wonder.  I think the vacuum motor is not working very well.  Even tough I made 28 miles per gallons at an average of 80 mph, I still think the mix of air and fuel is not very good.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||BlackARROW||04-30-2001||08:30 PM||||Fiero 2M4 '85||||reg||1
Z||000002||fyerstarter||04-30-2001||10:59 PM||||Your ECM should be able to compensate the amount of fuel to match your air intake up to a certain point. I'm not too sure from your description what the problem is but a cold air intakes is actually a performance modification and is a good thing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||04-30-2001||11:28 PM||||Thermac should only open under specific temperature conditions. If it is staying open all the time then the thermal control under the air cleaner is bad.<P>As for cold air intake..... <B>ALL</B> Original Equipment Fiero have cold air intake.<P>The sole purpose of Thermac is to prevent icing in the TBI. Depending on temperature and relative humidity this can happen well above freezing. <P>In warm weather the best way to kill thermac is to simply remove and cap the vacume line for it. then all you air will come from the side vent.<P>If the F/A ratio is signifigantly off then your ECM will usually tell you.<P>28mpg at that kind of speed isn't bad. I seriously doubt you have any real problem besides some intake noise.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Galen||04-30-2001||11:43 PM||||Ogre, what is this thermac thingie you speak of?<P>------------------<BR>Galen - 1988 Hawaiian Orchid Coupe - <A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero Website</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>Spinrpg.com - Free URL & e-mail Forwarding</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Pontiaddict||05-01-2001||12:18 AM||||It's the vacuum controlled flapper door thing on the air cleaner. <P>They used them on carbed motors to counteract the popping and sluggisness coused by the fuel not vaporising (along with the choke circuit) It also helped with cold motor fuel economy. Both were probably planned (though not really needed) for the duke and therefor Oreif's explaination is better.<P>||||reg||1
